-- title:
--   $R_{xz}=\bigotimes_iX^{x_i}Z^{z_i}$ is in the Pauli group $C_1$
-- statement:
--   For every $n$ and every outcome strings $x,z\in\{0,1\}^n$, the Pauli frame $R_{xz}=\bigotimes_{i}X^{x_i}Z^{z_i}$ is unitary and belongs to the $n$-qubit Pauli group
--
--   $$R_{xz}\in C_1=\mathcal P_n=\langle\, i^{c}\,\sigma_1\otimes\cdots\otimes\sigma_n \;:\; c\in\{0,1,2,3\},\ \sigma_j\in\{I,X,Y,Z\}\,\rangle .$$
--
--   This is Eq. (4)'s statement that the correction $R_{xz}$ is an operator in $C_1$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Membership is stated in the subgroup `QAlgorithms.pauliGroup n` of the unitary group, hence the existential over the unitarity proof. A factor with $x_i=z_i=1$ is $XZ=-iY$, so a phase is needed.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: R_xz, an operator in C1 which depends on the outcomes xz (not written out); for one qubit 'X, Y, or Z', 00 the identity → the explicit R_xz = ⊗_i X^{x_i} Z^{z_i} (Z first), with XZ = −iY for x_i = z_i = 1; reason: this is the frame the p. 1 Bell basis (first ket = teleported qubit) and |00⟩ + |11⟩ give, checked by hand and by a proved sanity check for n = 1; the paper's 'Y' is −iY up to a global phase
--   - reading: the Pauli group, 'comprised of tensor products of Pauli matrices, with overall sign ±1' (p. 2) → C1 = QAlgorithms.pauliGroup n, the group generated by tensor products of I, X, Y, Z with phases ±1, ±i; reason: the ±1-only set is not closed under products (XZ = −iY), and with it Eq. (4)'s 'R_xz is an operator in C1' fails for x_i = z_i = 1; C_k for k ≥ 2 is the same under either reading, since conjugation by U fixes scalars
--   - encoding: R_xz is an operator in C1 (a gate) → ∃ h : R_xz ∈ Matrix.unitaryGroup, ⟨R_xz, h⟩ ∈ pauliGroup n; reason: C1 is a subgroup of the unitary group in Lean, so membership carries the unitarity proof; every element of the paper's C1 is unitary, so no instance changes
-- source:
--   Gottesman, Chuang, Quantum teleportation is a universal computational primitive, arXiv:quant-ph/9908010v1, §II, Eq. (4), p. 3 ('Rxz is an operator in C1')

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Defs_Basic
import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Defs_QECC
import Definitions.Def_TeleportGates_Ck_pauliFrame

namespace TeleportGates.Ck

open QAlgorithms

/-- **The Pauli frame `R_{xz}` lies in `C₁`.**
Gottesman, Chuang, *Quantum teleportation is a universal computational primitive*,
arXiv:quant-ph/9908010v1, §II, Eq. (4), p. 3: "where Rxz is an operator in C1
which depends on the (random) Bell basis measurement outcomes xz".

For every `n` and every outcome strings `x z : Qubits n`, the matrix `R_{xz} = ⊗ᵢ X^{xᵢ} Z^{zᵢ}` is
unitary, and as a unitary it belongs to the `n`-qubit Pauli group `P_n` (tensor products of
`I, X, Y, Z` with phases `±1, ±i`).

**Formalization Note.** `C₁` is `QAlgorithms.pauliGroup n`, a subgroup of
`Matrix.unitaryGroup (Qubits n) ℂ`, hence the existential over the unitarity proof. Each factor
`X^{xᵢ} Z^{zᵢ}` is one of `I, Z, X, XZ = −iY`, so the phase `−i` is needed. -/
theorem pauliFrame_mem_pauliGroup (n : ℕ) (x z : Qubits n) :
    ∃ h : pauliFrame n x z ∈ Matrix.unitaryGroup (Qubits n) ℂ,
      (⟨pauliFrame n x z, h⟩ : Matrix.unitaryGroup (Qubits n) ℂ) ∈ pauliGroup n := by sorry

end TeleportGates.Ck
